      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We have $g(f(x)) = 3x+4$. Because $f(x)=ax+b$, we can rewrite that as $(ax+b)g(x) = 3x+4$ because $g(x) = 2x-5$. We can divide both sides by $x$ to get $ag(x) +bg(x) = 3$ which can be further simplified to $(a + b)g(x) =3$.  We are given that in every case $g(f(x)) = 3x+4$ so in particular $g(f(1)) = 3(1) +4$. We get $g(f(1)) = (a+b)(1-5) = -4(a+b)$. Setting those two equal we have $-4(a+b) = 2$ so $a+b = \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}$. The answer is -\\frac{1}{2}",
